window.docContents[87].resolve({"/Terms/#Cvc___lean----High-Level-API-for-CVC5--Terms--Untyped-Terms":{"contents":"A sort-erased cvc5 term.The term carries no sort on the Lean side: its sort is known to cvc5 alone, so a sort mistake, such\nas and applied to an integer, is reported by cvc5 at runtime. In exchange, terms of different\nsorts share one type, and an Array Term can hold them side by side.A term belongs to the Ω scope it was built in and cannot leave it. At runtime it is exactly a\ncvc5 term, so the wrapper costs nothing. typeCheck checks its sort against a Lean type and gives\nit that type as an index, producing a Cvc.Typed.Term.\n\nUntyped terms do not have a type parameter corresponding to their sort, which means untyped term\nconstructors do not provide any static guarantee that the result is a valid term. Instead, invalid\nterms will fail at runtime.\n\nA boolean literal.\n\nBinary conjunction.\n\nAn integer literal.\n\nUnlike the typed API, the sort of an untyped term for a function symbol cannot be inferred and must\nbe given explicitly. The first way to do so is to pass the actual sort.\n\nA symbol of the given sort: a fresh uninterpreted constant.\n\nThe second way is to pass the Lean type that we would use to create a typed term.\n\nA symbol of the sort α describes.mkSymbol takes the sort itself; this names it by the Lean type denoting it.\n\n\n\n","context":"Cvc.lean -- High-Level API for CVC5\u0009Terms","header":"4.2. Untyped Terms","id":"/Terms/#Cvc___lean----High-Level-API-for-CVC5--Terms--Untyped-Terms"}});